    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. My main concern with moving to LC is the initial setup period. When dealing with these types of old manuscripts, the cataloging process can often feel quite chaotic because many items aren't clearly defined or are missing key provenance. Can you elaborate on how each system handles 'unclassified' or disorganized entries during a transition? I'm worried about how we manage the items that don't fit neatly into a category right away.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Library of Congress Classification allows librarians to create subsidiary calls or temporary holding codes to accommodate uncharacterized or disordered manuscripts during a chaotic transition without losing access to them. In contrast, the Dewey Decimal System has fewer sub-divisions, meaning such items often have to wait indefinitely until full criteria are met or risk being discarded as non-filing material. For your specific project, leveraging the LC's broader flexibility to assign provisional codes is a safer strategy than rushing to fit ambiguous items into the rigid structure of Dewey Decimals.",
      "source": "target",
